#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064020 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064376 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0cf1bf7-69a3-50b2-adaa-84982ffac95e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Side Given the Rate of Change of the Volume"}] :question/id #uuid "1c2e1a0e-4e7b-5029-94c3-3eb14d8869bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-222" :question/problem "A cube is shrinking uniformly at a rate of $3\\,\\text{mm}^3/\\text{s}.$ How fast is each side of the cube shrinking when the volume of the cube is $27\\,\\text{mm}^3?$\n\n![](images/0c/0ca7021995b1a501f6cb862b33649bdb1927f7ce4a8630a9e261382a193f40a4.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the side of the cube and $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt}$ when $V=27\\,\\text{mm}^3.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\nV=x^3\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when $V = 27\\,\\text{mm}^3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{3} \\\\ 27 & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=3$ and $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm dt}=-3$ into the expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -3 & =3(3)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sides of the cube are shrinking at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{9}\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}$ at the moment when $x=3\\,\\textrm{mm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064021 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064377 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c29b3f9-5eac-5272-9715-f300211f91ad" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Surface Area Given the Rate of Change of a Side (and Vice Versa)"}] :question/id #uuid "74034d81-c90c-5ab4-b27c-aa1d7aca70cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-223" :question/problem "All of the edges of a cube are increasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}.$ How fast is the surface area of the cube changing when the volume of the cube is $8\\,\\text{cm}^3?$\n\n![](images/a6/a6ef2af1ed8e44f82469625e232ed67c0b809a3c8199a6ce52fd2bf2e3fcffa7.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the side length of the cube, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. All of the variables change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d S}{\\textrm dt}$ when $V= 8\\,\\text{cm}^3.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\nS= 6x^2\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}S & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when $V= 8\\,\\text{cm}^3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{3} \\\\ 8 & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=2\\,\\text{cm}=20\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nWe can now plug the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12(20)(2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =480.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cube's surface area is changing at a rate of  $480 \\,\\text{mm}^2/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064022 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064378 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814ed4fa-33c3-57eb-b843-6268c818fe36" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Volume Given the Rate of Change of a Side"}] :question/id #uuid "6694f63e-e83a-5597-81cb-80af3c132524" :question/math-academy-id "e-224" :question/problem "Water is being poured into a rectangular prism-shaped tank. The length and width of this tank are $20 \\textrm{ ft}$ and $15 \\textrm{ ft},$ respectively. If the height of the water increases at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{6}\\,\\textrm{ft/min}$, determine the rate at which the volume of stored water increases.\n\n![](images/ea/ea7c6749ec0585ab714640fa9155eba212e2a9c628b50cc3c3de07492b8b3aec.png)" :question/worked-solution "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L$ and $W$ be the length and width of the tank, respectively, and\n\n- let $h$ and $V$ be the height and volume of the water in the tank, respectively.\n\nNote that $h$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ and $W$ are constants. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nL=20\\,\\text{ft}, \\qquad  W=15\\,\\text{ft}, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt} = \\dfrac{1}{6}\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dV}{\\textrm dt}.$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $h,$ is given by the formula for the volume of the water in the tank:\n\n$$\nV = LWh\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides with respect to $t,$ treating both $L$ and $W$ as constants, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWh) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =LW\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(20)(15)\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =300\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is rising at a rate of $50\\,\\text{ft}^3\\textrm{/min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064724 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064378 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814ed4fa-33c3-57eb-b843-6268c818fe36" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Volume Given the Rate of Change of a Side"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d12cf7c-8e1b-5a17-ac3d-cf7f9d011685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400feet^{3}/hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062402} {:answer/id #uuid "ef55af14-bfb1-5c4f-891f-7767b9ae1e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "440feet^{3}/hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062403} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc02466-6589-5b3b-ae69-d8a5ef4a1fc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300feet^{3}/hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062404} {:answer/id #uuid "0f5e0fd7-5a5b-502d-9fbb-a7e15f19a1b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240feet^{3}/hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062405} {:answer/id #uuid "12645568-4761-55bd-897a-9f6a2987400d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360feet^{3}/hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062406}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f954cd2b-8873-5841-b890-5fd3add8628e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062401}] :question/id #uuid "51e84601-668c-5024-8729-dea441ebbfac" :question/math-academy-id "q-30022" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f504ea91bae5d27b3e7b31d697ed06c9e34d838724e8dafac46e69d84de9c3cf.png)\n\nA gas station has a rectangular gasoline storage tank stored underground. The tank is $15feet$ long and $10feet$ wide. A worker lowers a dip stick into the tank and notices that the depth of the gasoline is decreasing by $2feet$ every hour. How fast is the volume of gasoline decreasing?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064377 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c29b3f9-5eac-5272-9715-f300211f91ad" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Surface Area Given the Rate of Change of a Side (and Vice Versa)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "463b2dfe-12dc-5d8b-b485-bbdc7bf1bfc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375546} {:answer/id #uuid "a530aa11-ef3e-5bcd-8caf-52f9a0d11205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375547} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb143ef-2ae2-5c0f-ad9f-f168411366e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375548} {:answer/id #uuid "df3affe2-648f-55b4-a71c-2fce585166f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375549} {:answer/id #uuid "5406e375-01d7-556f-a3db-50d6681e14cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdb143ef-2ae2-5c0f-ad9f-f168411366e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375548} :answer-field/id #uuid "05433573-3744-5b22-9ee4-30694c12c8a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c144d86d-616b-51ad-b91f-c522e265b17b" :question/math-academy-id "q-86066" :question/problem "![](images/b2/b2158da0465250d14ce47909e7f1ef918c06c9721547a3533e1c65797cc9fab8.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $3\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ the width is increasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ and the $5\\,\\text{cm}$ height does not change. How fast is the surface area of the rectangular prism decreasing when its dimensions are $3\\,\\text{cm}$ in length and $10\\,\\text{cm}$ in width?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =10\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ z & =5\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-3\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x$ and $y.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nSince $z=5\\,\\text{cm}$ is not changing, we get\n\n$$\nS=10x+2xy+10y.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10x+2xy+10y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2x+10)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+(10+2y)(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2\\cdot 3+10)(5)+(10+2\\cdot 10)(-3) \\\\  & =80-90 \\\\  & =-10\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $10\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$"}]}
